<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long Turkish War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1593">1593</a>–<a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Thirteen Years' War (1593–1606), Fifteen Years' War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-habsburg-wars">Ottoman–Habsburg wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Long Turkish War was fought from 1593 to 1606 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, Wallachia and Moldavia joining the Habsburg side for a time. Neither side won decisively, despite the Ottoman victory at Mezőkeresztes in 1596. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok">Peace of Zsitvatorok</a> of 1606 treated the Habsburg emperor as an equal of the sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war.jpg" alt="Long Turkish War" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Folk art painting · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_war_1683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Skirmishes along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>–Ottoman border intensified from 1591, and in 1592 the Croatian fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihac">Bihać</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In the spring of 1593 Ottoman forces from the Eyalet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, and the Christian forces won the Battle of Sisak on 22 June 1593, which ended the Hundred Years' Croatian–Ottoman War.</p><p>The Long Turkish War itself began on 29 July 1593, when an Ottoman army under Sinan Pasha opened a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy with the Siege of Veszprém. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> the war is called the Fifteen Years' War, counted from the Ottoman campaign of 1591–1592 that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihac">Bihać</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> aimed to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the Habsburgs to recover the central territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> held by the Ottomans, and control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> line and its fortresses was crucial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> convened in 1594, declared a legal state of Türkenkriege and voted a tax grant that it renewed four years later and again in 1603. In 1595 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> organised an alliance, and Emperor Rudolf II and Sigismund Báthory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> signed a treaty of alliance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>; Aron Vodă of Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave">Michael the Brave</a> of Wallachia joined later that year.</p><p>Christian forces under Karl von Mansfeld captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> in 1595 but did not besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. Michael the Brave took forts along the Lower Danube from autumn 1594, and after an Ottoman counter-offensive of 100,000 men took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> and Târgoviște, he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Târgoviște, Bucharest and Giurgiu in October 1595.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Eger in 1596. At Mezőkeresztes, on 24–26 October 1596, a combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>–Transylvanian force of 45–50,000 men lost the battle after soldiers who thought they had won stopped fighting to plunder the Ottoman camp. The Habsburgs recaptured Győr and Komárom in 1598. In 1599 the Ottomans and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> allies raided towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a> and took thousands of people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The last phase of the war, from 1604 to 1606, was the uprising of Stephen Bocskai, who resisted Rudolf's prosecutions of noblemen. Declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a> on 21 February 1605 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 17 April 1605, he concluded the Treaty of Vienna with Rudolf in 1606. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok">Peace of Zsitvatorok</a> of 11 November 1606 brought meagre gains to the two main empires: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> won Eger and Kanizsa but gave the region of Vác, occupied since 1541, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p>It confirmed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> could not penetrate further into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> territory and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> was beyond Habsburg power. Rudolf presented the war as a victory, but he ended it deep in debt, and once peace was made the Habsburgs turned on one another in the Brothers' Quarrel between Rudolf and his brother Matthias.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Long Turkish War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The inconclusive <b>Long Turkish War</b> of 1593–1606 ended with the Peace of Zsitvatorok.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
